WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.722
Before a computer can improve how a chip is split, it needs a first split, called

00:00:05.722 --> 00:00:07.418
the initial bipartition.

00:00:07.418 --> 00:00:11.869
You will meet three ways to build one: random, greedy and grow.

00:00:11.869 --> 00:00:15.896
On one small graph, they give cuts from three to sixteen.

00:00:15.896 --> 00:00:20.064
The habit that fixes it: try several starts, keep the best.

00:00:20.064 --> 00:00:25.459
A chip is made of small building blocks called cells, joined by wires.

00:00:25.459 --> 00:00:28.620
Our example has five cells, named A to E.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:32.088
Some pairs share many wires, others only one.

00:00:32.088 --> 00:00:35.485
We draw each cell as a dot, called a node.

00:00:35.485 --> 00:00:38.397
A line between two nodes is an edge.

00:00:38.397 --> 00:00:43.089
Its number, the weight, counts the wires between the pair.

00:00:43.089 --> 00:00:47.376
A, B and D, E are the heaviest, with five wires each.

00:00:47.376 --> 00:00:51.482
Splitting the cells into two sides is called a bipartition.

00:00:51.482 --> 00:00:56.006
Edges that cross between the two sides are cut, and drawn in red.

00:00:56.006 --> 00:01:00.600
The cutsize adds up their weights: here, two plus one makes three.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.018
Putting every cell on one side gives a cut of zero, but that split is useless.

00:01:06.018 --> 00:01:09.978
So we also check balance: how many cells each side holds.

00:01:09.978 --> 00:01:14.424
A split is legal when no side holds more than three of the five.

00:01:14.424 --> 00:01:18.873
Labs four and five teach two famous methods, called KL and FM.

00:01:18.873 --> 00:01:23.465
They improve a split by moving cells from one side to the other.

00:01:23.465 --> 00:01:26.837
But KL and FM need a legal split to start from.

00:01:26.837 --> 00:01:32.720
Think of picking two teams: first any two teams, then swaps to make the game fair.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.021
A good first split leaves less swapping to do.

00:01:36.021 --> 00:01:41.617
Pause and guess: could a split made at random be as good as this one, with cut

00:01:41.617 --> 00:01:42.048
three?
